      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The historic Marsteller Covered Bridge, a wooden span crossing Blacklick Creek, is a notable landmark in the community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://nearbylens.com/cities/usa/pa/marsteller/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Founded in 1902 as a joint venture of coal companies, the town reorganized in 1908 under T.H. leadership.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/marsteller_pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early settlers were German immigrants who farmed the Moss Creek valley before the coal boom reshaped the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/marsteller_pennsylvania",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Colliery 22 of the Pennsylvania Coal and Coke Corporation operated in Marsteller until 1948, with teams competing in statewide safety contests.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/marsteller_pennsylvania",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Today Marsteller lies within Barr Township, part of the Johnstown metro, where the legacy of coal mining still shapes the landscape.",
